Each recursive call works on one starting position. It reverses up to `k` characters, skips the next `k`, and then calls itself for the next block. The `Math.min` call makes the last short block work correctly.

Dry run for `"abcdefg"`, `k = 2`:

- Position `0`: reverse `ab` to `ba`.
- Position `4`: reverse `ef` to `fe`.
- Position `8`: stop because it is outside the string.

```java
class Solution {
    public String reverseStr(String s, int k) {
        char[] chars = s.toCharArray();
        reverseBlocks(chars, 0, k);
        return new String(chars);
    }

    private void reverseBlocks(char[] chars, int start, int k) {
        if (start >= chars.length) {
            return;
        }

        int left = start;
        int right = Math.min(start + k - 1, chars.length - 1);

        while (left < right) {
            char temp = chars[left];
            chars[left] = chars[right];
            chars[right] = temp;
            ++left;
            --right;
        }

        reverseBlocks(chars, start + 2 * k, k);
    }
}
```

An empty string returns immediately. The recursive calls add stack space, so this is less space-friendly than a loop.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n / k)` for the recursion stack, plus `O(n)` for the character array.

The loop jumps by `2 * k`, because only those positions begin a section that must change. Two pointers then move toward the center of the first `k` characters.

The final section may be shorter than `k`. Using an exclusive end index with `min` avoids reading past the string and reverses all remaining characters when needed.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <string>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string reverseStr(string s, int k) {
        for (int start = 0; start < static_cast<int>(s.size()); start += 2 * k) {
            int end = min(start + k, static_cast<int>(s.size()));

            for (int left = start, right = end - 1; left < right;
                 ++left, --right) {
                char temp = s[left];
                s[left] = s[right];
                s[right] = temp;
            }
        }

        return s;
    }
};
```

The input is edited in place. When `k` is larger than the string, the loop runs once and reverses the complete string. The problem constraints keep `k` positive, so the step never becomes zero.

Time complexity: `O(n)` because each character belongs to at most one reversed section.

Space complexity: `O(1)` besides the string returned by value.

Instead of swapping characters, this version builds the answer one block at a time. For each `2k` section, the first part is reversed and the following part is copied as it is.

If the last section has fewer than `k` characters, reversing that shorter slice does exactly what is needed. If it has between `k` and `2k` characters, only the first `k` are reversed.

```javascript
class Solution {
    reverseStr(s, k) {
        let result = [];

        for (let start = 0; start < s.length; start += 2 * k) {
            const firstPart = s.slice(start, start + k);
            const secondPart = s.slice(start + k, start + 2 * k);

            result.push(firstPart.split('').reverse().join(''));
            result.push(secondPart);
        }

        return result.join('');
    }
}
```

For `s = "abc"` and `k = 5`, `firstPart` contains the whole string, so the result is `"cba"`. This method is readable, but it creates several temporary strings.

Time complexity: `O(n)` overall.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the result and temporary pieces.

A Python string cannot be edited directly, so it is first changed into a list of characters. The list is split into pieces of `2 * k`, and the first `k` characters of each piece are replaced by their reversed order.

This keeps the code short and lets Python handle a final piece that is smaller than `k` without a separate special case.

```python
class Solution:
    def reverseStr(self, s: str, k: int) -> str:
        chars = list(s)

        for start in range(0, len(chars), 2 * k):
            chars[start:start + k] = chars[start:start + k][::-1]

        return "".join(chars)
```

The list uses extra memory, but the slice boundaries make the remaining-character cases easy to handle.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(n)` for the character list and temporary slices.

The string is changed in groups of `2 * k`. At the start of each group, only the next `k` characters are reversed. The rest of that group is skipped.

The right pointer is limited by the string length, so a short final group is safe. For example, with `s = "abcdefg"` and `k = 2`, the groups are `abcd` and `efg`. Reversing `ab` and then `ef` gives `bacdfeg`.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string reverseStr(string s, int k) {
        int n = static_cast<int>(s.size());

        for (int start = 0; start < n; start += 2 * k) {
            int left = start;
            int right = min(start + k - 1, n - 1);

            while (left < right) {
                swap(s[left], s[right]);
                ++left;
                --right;
            }
        }

        return s;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.

Space complexity: `O(1)` apart from the returned string. The input string is modified inside the method.

